     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 5,100 mg/day of metformin is about twice the maximum daily dose. It adds no glycemic benefit and carries a real risk of severe lactic acidosis. Metformin doses at this level are in the range seen in overdose. I'm not comfortable placing it, and I can't verify the clinical reasoning behind it.\n\nThis isn't a judgment about your authority. You can still place the order directly through the standard order-entry interface. It would go through pharmacist verification, which is the right safeguard for an out-of-range dose.\n\nI can enter one of these now:\n- **Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** This is 2,550 mg/day, the maximum for immediate-release.\n- **Metformin 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals.** This is 2,000 mg/day.\n- **Another dose within the labeled range.** Tell me the dose and frequency.\n\nIf it would help, I can add a note to the order, such as \"titrate per glucose response\" or \"pharmacy to review.\""
      }
